WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.310
In this video, we'll look at how to build terrain using the

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:03.780
Unity Terrain Tools package and the

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.870
Universal Render Pipeline. The

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.320


00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.850
Terrain Tools are an excellent way to create high-performance worlds for many platforms with exceptional flexibility and

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.772
creative freedom. They work in

00:00:13.772 --> 00:00:16.870


00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:18.953
conjunction with other Unity systems and serve as an entry point

00:00:18.953 --> 00:00:22.448
to creating various types of games and simulations.

00:00:22.448 --> 00:00:24.910


00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.970
Once you're familiar with the Terrain Tools, you'll see that it's a

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.077
fantastic way to build worlds. It

00:00:32.077 --> 00:00:33.918


00:00:33.918 --> 00:00:35.537


00:00:35.537 --> 00:00:36.620


00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.600
provides many features to define your project style and create highly polished scenes. To get started with the

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.205
latest Terrain Tools in Unity 2020.1, you

00:00:41.205 --> 00:00:43.240


00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.214


00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:48.580
need to make sure your Package Manager is set to enable preview packages. This is under

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:53.580
[Project Settings] &gt; [Package
Manager] &gt; [Advanced Settings]. In

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:54.793
this demo, we'll also install several other

00:00:54.793 --> 00:00:57.820
free terrain assets from the Asset Store.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.240


00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.110
Check the description for a list of these packages. Once you've

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.176
installed the Terrain Tools, you can

00:01:05.176 --> 00:01:07.530
start building terrain.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.500
Select [Window] &gt; [Terrain] &gt; [TerrainToolbox].

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.090
Dock the Terrain Toolbox and

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:16.270
go to the [Create New Terrain] tab. The

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.750
tab shows you setting options such as size, height, and starting position. Let's

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.750


00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:26.760
create one terrain with all the default settings at the

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.620
bottom of the window.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:29.950
Clicking [Create] will

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.280
display a new terrain in your scene Raising and

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.030
lowering the terrain is the most important component for editing it

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.780
using the terrain mesh selected in the Hierarchy window

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.960


00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.320
Make sure the Inspector is displayed

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.390
At the top of the terrain component you will see

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.800
several icons The

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.170
brush icon allows you to

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:55.490
directly edit the terrain geometry texture

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.150
Below the row of icons

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.060
Click the dropdown and select [Raise or
Lower Terrain]

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.460
Select a simple round brush mask

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.110
Now you are ready to modify the mesh Let's

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.850
create some basic hills and raised areas Without

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.850
obvious steps To

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.880
create more detailed terrain The

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.660
terrain tool supports 16-bit depth brushes

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:18.782
Many of them are

00:02:18.782 --> 00:02:22.240
included in the terrain tool sample asset pack

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.760
Links are provided in the description

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.830
Here is an example of a working brush

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:30.046
Holding down A on your keyboard

00:02:30.046 --> 00:02:32.820
adjusts the strength of the sculpt brush

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.610
S changes the size of the brush D rotates the brush Holding down

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:38.780
Shift

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.330
changes the brush's behavior to smoothing Holding down

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.170
Ctrl inverts the brush Let's

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:47.600
lower the terrain with a brush mask instead of raising it

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.160
If you want to change these keybindings, you can

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.783
use Unity's shortcut manager to rebound

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.820
Using a noise brush  You can

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.740
create a lot of high-frequency detail across the terrain. You can

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.593
add a little randomness to the terrain mesh. The

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.352
latest terrain tools also include an erosion brush that

00:03:10.352 --> 00:03:13.087
can simulate the effects of wind, water, and heat changes on the mesh. When

00:03:13.087 --> 00:03:16.163


00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.710
used in combination with other brush parameters, you

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:23.803
can create a lot of very realistic effects.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.730
Digging further into the tools,

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.030
we polished the mask filters.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.273
These powerful brush filters allow you to

00:03:32.273 --> 00:03:34.917
parameterize strokes and

00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:39.003
create multi-layer inputs that make it easy to create dynamic effects.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.257


00:03:43.257 --> 00:03:46.460
Once you have the terrain and feel that the rough placement of the features is right, let's

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.670
move on to texturing.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.570
One of the great things about the Unity terrain workflow is that once you have a

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.300
good understanding of the texture system, you can

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.230
easily iterate, adjust, and improve any part of the mesh at any time. You can

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.000


00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.570
then start refining and bring your

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.373
scene to life.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.300
Creating a palette of terrain materials in the Layers tab

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.190
greatly simplifies painting the terrain.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:14.630
"Experience speaks for itself," but it is important to understand that the

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.438
order of textures can be easily changed.

00:04:17.438 --> 00:04:19.510


00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.874
However, if the

00:04:21.874 --> 00:04:24.467
order of terrain meshes is changed after you have started texturing, you may encounter

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:27.430
visual problems.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:29.460
Having a palette prepared in advance

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.080
allows you to easily revert materials. In

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.400
case you make a mistake,

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.352
keep the order of materials in mind.

00:04:36.352 --> 00:04:39.283
When painting textures, you need to understand that you are actually painting weighted indices

00:04:39.283 --> 00:04:43.340
onto a splatmap.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.400
URP's terrain shader

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.710
can blend between an unlimited number of terrain layers;

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.650
however, with height-based blending enabled,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:55.930
Unity supports four terrain layers and one splatmap. You can

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:58.473
save texture palettes as presets to

00:04:58.473 --> 00:05:00.610


00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:03.023
easily set up terrain meshes with similar biomes and features. The

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.168
terrain material itself is a

00:05:06.168 --> 00:05:09.938


00:05:09.938 --> 00:05:14.370
custom material type that supports albedo, normal mapping, and a special mask texture with multiple features. The

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.920
mask texture supports metallic

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.360
values ​​in the red channel,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.810
ambient occlusion values ​​in the green channel,

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.060
height values ​​which greatly improve material blending in the

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.620
blue channel, and

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.190
smoothness values ​​in the alpha channel.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.740
If you choose not to include a mask channel, there are

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:35.330
metallic and smoothness overrides for the entire material. The

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:36.730


00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:39.716
height value only affects the material if height-based blending is enabled.

00:05:39.716 --> 00:05:41.470


00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.980
Crucial to the appearance of the material is the tiling setting, which

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:47.497
includes texture size and offset, offset based on a

00:05:47.497 --> 00:05:50.504
scalar amount related to texture size.

00:05:50.504 --> 00:05:52.103


00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:54.970
Once the basic setup is complete, you can

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.240
start defining features. In

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.480
this example, we are creating a temperate forest,

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:04.450
set on a gentle hill with clearly defined paths.  There's

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:08.230
greenwash to define areas that are mainly covered in trees, and also

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.970
areas that are covered in moss. For

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:13.020
the darkest and wettest parts of the scene, there are

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.133


00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:17.350
rocky patches that gather at the bottom of a watershed, cliff patches for more vertical sections of

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:20.580
terrain where grass and moss are less likely to grow,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.800


00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.980
and some muddy and soily materials.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.317


00:06:27.317 --> 00:06:30.100
For sections with more foot movement, such as people and wildlife, there are

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.770
damp and depressions at the edges and

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.886
dry and cracked textures in the middle.

00:06:35.886 --> 00:06:38.644
Whatever type of scene you are creating, you want to

00:06:38.644 --> 00:06:40.230
think like nature and

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.332


00:06:43.332 --> 00:06:45.172
take into account how the basic elements interact with the terrain. Ask yourself where

00:06:45.172 --> 00:06:48.553
the sun hits, where the water flows, where the rocks roll.

00:06:48.553 --> 00:06:51.260


00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.710
Combined with quality references, you can really begin to understand the

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:56.210
logical and harmonious relationships between the scene elements. The

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:59.633


00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:04.350


00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.712
next step to make the scene more believable, with the terrain together with the textures underneath, is to

00:07:07.712 --> 00:07:11.320
add trees and other vegetation to the terrain. In the

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.162
terrain component,

00:07:13.162 --> 00:07:16.750
here we go from the brush palette to the tree palette.

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:19.400
First click [Edit Trees].

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.080
Add some speed trees. There

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.230
is also a link in the description box.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.730
Since we are building this scene for the desktop

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.420


00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:32.720
Select the Broadleaf and Conifer desktops as source models.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:34.210
Before placing the trees,

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:36.550
increase the random size range and

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.058
uncheck the lock width and height to add

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:42.900
more variation to the tree silhouettes. Make sure to

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.900
keep the values ​​within a reasonable range

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.287
so you don't notice any unnatural deformations of the trunks and branches.

00:07:48.287 --> 00:07:50.430


00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.003
Next,

00:07:52.003 --> 00:07:53.800
select the broadleaf tree prefab from the tree palette.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.372
Click the Mass Place Trees button and

00:07:56.372 --> 00:07:58.800
quickly add it to the terrain. A

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.970


00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:03.560
dialog box will appear with the number of trees for a 250,000 cubic meter zone.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.863
1,500 to 2,000 trees is

00:08:05.863 --> 00:08:10.830
roughly the right number for a healthy forest in this vast area. If you use the primary directional light with the

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:13.140
first tree type added, the

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.770


00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:19.250
trees will have very strong black shadows, which is

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.580
usually undesirable visually.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.575
This

00:08:24.575 --> 00:08:28.120
happens because the URP does not support global illumination or reflection probes for trees. To mitigate this, add some directional bounce lights

00:08:29.778 --> 00:08:32.210
as children of the primary directional light.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:35.230


00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:38.210
These will help reduce the obvious strong shadows and

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:42.290
enhance the shapes beneath the trees.

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:45.900


00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:49.113
Make sure the overall value of the light tone is low to match the overall color of the terrain. Make some changes

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.297
to the size and density of the tree brush.

00:08:53.297 --> 00:08:55.120


00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.260
Then you can move around within the terrain mesh,

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.905
rearrange the trees, and control your clicks to remove trees

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:01.590
from undesirable locations. You can

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.740


00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.003
use normal click strokes to

00:09:06.003 --> 00:09:08.660
add trees where they weren't before.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:11.580
Once you have the deciduous trees arranged as you like, you can

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.380
select conifers and add variety to your forest

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:14.688
with this combination of shapes. In

00:09:14.688 --> 00:09:17.920


00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:19.210
your own work, you might

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:20.602


00:09:20.602 --> 00:09:23.530
want to experiment with different tree combinations at various stages of maturity, but even just

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.368
these two variations will

00:09:25.368 --> 00:09:26.803
give you a nice look. Once the

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:31.330
trees are done and the area around the path is neatly shaped, you

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:35.440
can move on to the grass system for the final touches. In the

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.690
demo, we'll keep it simple and

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:41.120
use billboards of grass and flower textures.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:42.423
Add them to the palette, and once that's

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.120
done, the

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.240
workflow is similar to painting the tree arrangement.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:54.160
Variety is important so you don't make sections too dense. You need enough vegetation to

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.493
break up the tree trunks and bring the

00:09:55.493 --> 00:09:58.170
shape of the hills to life. Even

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:00.670


00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.178
pops of color with flowers or

00:10:02.178 --> 00:10:04.073
drier-colored grass will make the

00:10:04.073 --> 00:10:06.055


00:10:06.055 --> 00:10:09.010
whole scene much more vibrant without much overhead.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:11.420
This is where references from photographs and paintings help

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.330
guide your arrangement and

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.345


00:10:15.345 --> 00:10:19.090
find the best places to densely place the billboards.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:21.490
Unity also supports detail meshes,

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:23.662
so you can create

00:10:23.662 --> 00:10:28.662
fully 3D leaves and rocks in a very dynamic way.  You can add other items to the terrain. Once

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:32.773
all the setup is complete and the basic form is finished, you can now spend some time actually moving around in the scene using all the tools we've introduced to

00:10:32.773 --> 00:10:34.827
polish the scene to a higher level and

00:10:34.827 --> 00:10:37.743
start testing potential gameplay. Once

00:10:37.743 --> 00:10:40.380


00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.743


00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:47.010
the world is set up, you

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:48.690


00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.060
can quickly add a wind zone to bring the trees to life.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.980
Keep the settings low,

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.961
otherwise it will look like a hurricane simulation.

00:10:55.961 --> 00:10:58.393
Detail can be very impressive.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:06.120


00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.480
Add similarly detailed post-processing to improve the final image quality and

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:10.680
ensure you get the mood you want.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.548
Take some time to look around your creation in play mode and evaluate

00:11:13.548 --> 00:11:16.870
what you've been able to create using the terrain tools. For more

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:18.500


00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:21.040
information about terrain tools, please

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:22.430
see the link below.

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:23.473
Thank you for watching.

